 le-monde\">\n          <div class=\"masthead monde\">Le Monde</div>\n          <div class=\"thick-rule\"></div>\n\n          <div class=\"copy-grid\">\n            <div class=\"column\">\n              <h3 class=\"article-title\">Allemagne<br>v. Etats-<br>Unis</h3>\n\n              <p>The green field at Parc des Princes shimmered like the lips of the virgin spring, unmistakably French, inno&shy;cent yet knowing, a totality bursting upon a completion. Across its surface, 22 men—non-French, non-signifi&shy;cant—scampered after a black and white ball, their existence overshadowed by the deep wisdom of the French soil, the chic of the grass. This reporter sat in the press box munching a biscuit, remem&shy;bering the soft smells of his nursemaid so many years ago in Deauville.</p>\n\n              <p>As the game commenced, the score was nothingness to nothingness. The ball was</p>\n            </div>\n\n            <div class=\"column\">\n              <p class=\"first\">round with that marvelous roundness that round things have when they are in France. The Germans, paradoxes of angst and <em>Anschluss</em>, pushed from East to West (ironic echo of their Austrian cousins at Austerlitz!). Meanwhile, the Americans, vulgar, com&shy;mercial, yet vital in their childish way, stood hapless before the Hessian onslaught. The score may have changed as the match progressed, but is a goal an addition or a sub&shy;traction? Is it not a slavish concession to the bourgeois production ethic? Does it not mar the Hellenic perfection of the spheric zero? Is a ball in the back of the net a mere construct played upon by the ever-shifting fancy of our per&shy;ceptions? Up in the press box, I sneezed, and the entire Yale English faculty got a cold.&hellip;</p>\n\n              <div class=\"signature\">\n                Pierre Henri-Lévy Derrida<br>\n                <span class=\"date\">(Mercredi 27 mai.)</span>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n        </article>\n\n        <article class=\"article washington\">\n          <div class=\"masthead wash\">The Washington Times</div>\n          <div class=\"thick-rule\"></div>\n          <p class=\"byline\">BY PATRICK J. BUCHANAN</p>\n\n          <div class=\"copy-grid\">\n            <div class=\"column\">\n              <h3 class=\"article-title\">United<br>States v.<br>Germany</h3>\n\n              <p class=\"no-indent\">American soldiers with names like O’Brien and O’Malley didn’t die at Ver&shy;dun and Bastogne so a bunch of Huns with names like Klinsmann and Mattheus could shoot soc&shy;cer balls past American goalies. But, my friends, that’s where America’s elites have led us—into the clutches of a bunch of thick-thighed Hessians with devious elbows.</p>\n\n              <p>It may not occur to the pansy mandarins of the corporate class, but Ameri&shy;can greatness was built on the premise that God gave us arms, and we should use them. The rest of the world is filled with dim-witted dribblers—half of whom don’t even have last names—who no sooner see a ball flying through the air than they want to stick their foreheads in its way. Meanwhile, their hordes of</p>\n            </div>\n\n            <div class=\"column\">\n              <p class=\"first\">fans—whose lives consist of riots interrupted by wel&shy;fare checks—jump up and down screeching their idi&shy;otic anthems. The full lyrics of one of their soccer songs is this: “Oh-lay, oh-lay, oh-lay, oh-lay.” They sound like a bunch of ban&shy;shees worshiping a potato chip.</p>\n\n              <p>So the nation of Mark McGwire and Michael Jor&shy;dan finds itself sucking up to Yugoslavia and the Netherlands in this qua&shy;drennial soccerfest. How did we get into this posi&shy;tion? The answer is that the World Cup is the sports equivalent of the GATT: big, multicultural, run by hairless global bureaucrats, and organized to create the greatest possible humilia&shy;tion for the American working man. And the global elites, who check their loyalties at the airport Gold Club lounges, want to merge our identity into some cosmopolitan cock&shy;tail reception. Whenever I see an American calling this game “football,” I want to puke.</p>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n        </article>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n  </main>\n</body>\n</html>"
